    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2061044735201235351",
      "url": "https://x.com/_lonewolf____/status/2061044735201235351",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/_lonewolf____/status/2061044735201235351",
      "text": "The image captures a magnificent, mid-20th-century street view of Avenida Rizal (Rizal Avenue) in Santa Cruz, Manila. \n\nIt highlights the avenue during its undisputed golden age as the premier commercial and cultural nerve center of the Philippines. \n\nLooking down the bustling street, one can see iconic landmarks like the vertical sign of the Ideal Theatre, the nearby Goodearth Department Store, an array of classic mid-century vehicles, public transit buses, jeepneys, and throngs of pedestrians crossing the clean, well-organized thoroughfare.\n\nThe history of Avenida Rizal is a story of transformation from a quiet colonial road to the glittering \"Broadway of Manila,\" followed by a dramatic period of urban decay and modern adaptation.\n\n1. Origins and Consolidation (Early 1900s)\n\nBefore it became a massive boulevard, the route consisted of two separate, narrow streets during the Spanish colonial era: Calle Salcedo and Calle Dulumbayan.\n\nFollowing the arrival of American colonial rule, urban planners recognized the need for a major north-south traffic artery passing through the commercial district of Santa Cruz. \n\nIn 1911, by virtue of a city ordinance, these two streets were integrated and extended to form a single, continuous thoroughfare. The avenue was officially named Rizal Avenue in honor of the country's national hero, Dr. José Rizal.\n\n2. The Golden Age: The \"Broadway of Manila\" (1930s–1960s)\n\nBy the 1930s, and continuing into the post-World War II reconstruction era, Avenida Rizal transformed into the unrivaled entertainment and shopping capital of the country.\n\nAvenida Rizal was globally renowned for its concentration of grand, standalone luxury cinemas. Architectural masterpieces designed by National Artists such as the Ideal Theatre (prominently featured on the left in the photo), the Avenue, State, Ever, and Scala theaters lined the avenue. These theaters featured advanced amenities like central air conditioning and held exclusive screening rights for major Hollywood studios like MGM, Warner Bros., and Fox.\n\nBefore mega-malls existed, the country's high-end shopping happened here. The avenue was packed with upscale department stores like Goodearth (visible just past the Ideal sign), transactional banks, jewelers, bookshops, and high-end restaurants. It was a clean, prestigious promenade where visiting downtown was treated as a formal social event.\n\nThe 1980s marked the beginning of a sharp decline for Avenida Rizal. The concept of commercial entertainment rapidly shifted to suburban mega-malls, drawing businesses and high-end consumers away from downtown Manila.\n\nThe final blow to the avenue’s traditional glamour came with the construction of the Light Rail Transit Line 1 (LRT-1). The massive, elevated concrete train tracks were built directly above the center of Rizal Avenue.\n\n The construction cast a permanent shadow over the street, making it dark, claustrophobic, and prone to trapped vehicle emissions.\n\n The elegant boulevard quickly devolved into a noisy, heavily congested corridor plagued by air and noise pollution.\n\nLandmark single-screen cinemas lost their clientele, fell into neglect, and were either demolished (like the Ideal Theatre) or converted into low-end stalls.\n\nIn an effort to rescue the area from decay, the Manila city government under Mayor Lito Atienza executed a radical revitalization project in 2003, pedestrianizing a major section of Avenida Rizal from Carriedo to Recto. The road was paved over with tiles, traffic was diverted, and open-air shopping was encouraged.",
